          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 病人",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "病人 — bìngrén — \"patient; sick person\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "病人想喝水 bìngrén xiǎng hē shuǐ — The patient would like some water."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 病 (bìng) — outside, the sickbed piece of five strokes: a dot, a bar, a long fall down the left, then a dot and a rising stroke on the left side; inside it, five strokes: a bar, a box with a hooked right side, and a small 人 in the box. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "病人 (bìngrén) is built from 病 (bìng) 'illness' and 人 'person', both inherited from Middle Chinese, bjaengH and nyin (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the dictionary glosses the first 'grief' and 'suffering', and quotes an older book: an illness grown worse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
